<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">{{ site.researchByUrl[page.url].kicker }}</p>
  <h1>Two Inversions</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A Möbius circuit reverses a local transverse frame. A second circuit restores it.
    A perfect optical inverter can do the same. <strong>Does residue of a single inversion make it through
    after a second inversion?</strong></p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The short answer</p>
    <h2 id="t-answer">Not If &ldquo;Inversion&rdquo; Means an Exact Involution</h2>
    <p>Let \(P\) be the operation and suppose \(P^2=I\). Then every state in the model satisfies</p>
    <p class="formula">\[x\xmapsto{P}Px\xmapsto{P}P^2x=x.\]</p>
    <p>For an observable \(O\), the direct double-inversion residue is therefore</p>
    <p class="formula">\[\Delta_O=O(P^2x)-O(x)=0.\]</p>
    <p>Any non-zero remainder must live somewhere the ideal state map did not erase: a path-dependent phase,
      a larger state space, a changed boundary, a dissipative material, an environment, or a record. Calling
      the remainder &ldquo;Möbius memory&rdquo; does not provide that mechanism.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Established topology <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h2 id="t-moebius-frame">What the Möbius Strip Reverses</h2>
      <p>Write a rectangular strip with the identification</p>
      <p class="formula">\[(0,v)\sim(2\pi,-v).\]</p>
      <p>After one circuit, a continuously transported transverse frame returns reversed. After two circuits
        it returns to its starting orientation. This is the non-orientability of the surface. It is not an
        automatic reversal of electric charge, time, matter or causality.</p>
      <p>The robust remainder is global: no continuous choice of orientation exists everywhere on the strip.
        That topological fact survives even though a transported frame is restored after two circuits.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Established optics <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h2 id="t-polarization">What an Optical Element Reverses</h2>
      <p>A monochromatic pure polarization state can be represented by a Jones vector. An ideal half-wave
        plate with fast axis \(\theta\) has, up to convention and an overall phase,</p>
      <p class="formula">\[H(\theta)=R(-\theta)
        \begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}R(\theta).\]</p>
      <p>It reflects a linear polarization angle as \(\alpha\mapsto2\theta-\alpha\). Passing through the same
        ideal plate twice gives \(H(\theta)^2=I\), up to a global phase. Two different plates need not cancel;
        order, axis angle, retardance and wavelength all matter.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

