<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A necessary optical subtlety</p>
    <h2 id="t-polarity">Polarity Is Not One Binary Switch</h2>
    <p>Light polarization includes linear, circular and elliptical states. Linear angles are identified
      modulo \(\pi\), while the Stokes vector places pure states on the Poincaré sphere. Jones matrices act on
      amplitudes; their corresponding rotations act on Stokes vectors. An overall complex phase is invisible
      to an isolated intensity measurement but can become a relative phase in an interferometer.</p>
    <p>A quarter-wave plate is not an order-two inversion: two identical quarter-wave passages make a
      half-wave retardance, not generally the identity. A mirror may also change handedness conventions and
      propagation direction. Any comparison with a Möbius circuit must therefore name the optical operation,
      the coordinate convention and the measured observable.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Five possible meanings of residue</p>
    <h2 id="t-residue-ledger">Where Something Could Survive</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card"><h3>1 &middot; Topological holonomy <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></h3><p>A local frame is restored after two Möbius circuits, while the surface remains globally non-orientable. The &ldquo;residue&rdquo; is a property of the bundle or space, not a leftover material.</p></article>
    <article class="frame card"><h3>2 &middot; Geometric phase <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></h3><p>A cyclic polarization path can acquire a Pancharatnam&ndash;Berry phase determined by geometry on the Poincaré sphere. It is observed relative to a reference path, not as a failure of the final polarization state to return.</p></article>
    <article class="frame card"><h3>3 &middot; Hysteresis or dissipation <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></h3><p>A real optical element can heat, age, saturate or respond nonlinearly. The second operation then differs from the first. This is material memory, not ideal inversion algebra.</p></article>
    <article class="frame card"><h3>4 &middot; Decoherence or environment <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></h3><p>The selected degree of freedom may return while correlations leak into uncontrolled modes. A larger state description is required to locate the record.</p></article>
    <article class="frame card"><h3>5 &middot; TTI inherited residue <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></h3><p>TTI may propose a boundary variable \(r\) with its own update law, such as \((x,r)\mapsto(Px,r+f[x])\). That enlarged model can retain memory even when \(P^2x=x\), but the law and coupling must be derived and tested.</p></article>
    <article class="frame card illuminated"><h3>The forbidden shortcut</h3><p>&ldquo;It inverted, therefore it remembers&rdquo; is not a mechanism. Exact restoration in the declared state space is the null result; memory requires an independently measurable carrier.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A transformation you can calculate</p>
    <h2 id="t-worked-involution">Why Doing an Exact Inversion Twice Restores the Modelled State</h2>
    <p>Begin with a simple arrow in a plane, represented by \(v=(x,y)\). Reflection across the horizontal
      axis sends it to \(P v=(x,-y)\). Apply the same reflection again and the second minus sign cancels the
      first: \(P(Pv)=(x,y)=v\). In matrix form,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      P=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},
      \qquad P^2=I.
    \]</p>
    <p>A map satisfying \(P^2=I\) is an involution. Once the transformation and state space have been fully
      specified, exact return is not a tendency or an approximation; it is the algebraic result. A claim that
      some residue must remain therefore cannot come from the inversion alone. It must introduce an additional
      variable, a path-dependent phase, imperfect apparatus or an environment that was omitted from \(v\).</p>
    <p>The phrase “opposite polarity” can hide several different operations. Reversing a spatial arrow,
      exchanging two labels, changing electric charge, taking a complex conjugate and rotating optical
      polarisation are not one universal switch. Their squares and observable consequences differ. Before two
      inversions can be combined, both must act on the same declared object or a lawful map between their
      state spaces must be supplied.</p>
    <p class="lede">If the enlarged state changes, there may be memory. If only the declared state is measured
      and it returns exactly, calling an unseen remainder “residue” adds a conclusion rather than finding one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">State and journey are different questions</p>
    <h2 id="t-phase-reading">How an Optical State Can Return While Its Path Is Still Measurable</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Final polarisation</h3>
      <p>A Jones vector represents the polarisation of coherent light. A calibrated optical transformation can
        return that vector after a cycle. Full polarimetry then finds the same measurable polarisation at the
        end, within experimental error. For that degree of freedom, there is no leftover inversion.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Relative phase</h3>
      <p>Interference compares the returned beam with a reference that followed another route. The test beam
        may acquire a dynamic or geometric phase even when its polarisation returns. That phase is relational:
        it is visible through interference, not as a second hidden polarisation.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Apparatus memory</h3>
      <p>A real element can warm, deform, saturate or couple light into unmeasured modes. Reversing the nominal
        control may not reverse those changes. The apparatus and environment then carry history. This is a
        physical effect to characterise, not evidence that exact inversion algebra failed.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Topological path</h3>
      <p>Parallel transport around a non-trivial loop can produce holonomy: a frame returns to its point with a
        transformation determined by the route. The effect belongs to the connection and path. It must not be
        inferred merely from drawing a Möbius strip beside an optical component.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A familiar analogy, with its limit stated</p>
    <h2 id="t-walk-analogy">Coming Home Does Not Mean That No Journey Happened</h2>
    <p>A walker can leave home, circle a hill and return to the same door. Position has been restored, while
      elapsed time, fatigue and memory distinguish the return from the departure. There is no contradiction
      because position was never the complete state. Add a clock, energy store and memory record, and the
      enlarged state plainly does not return.</p>
    <p>The analogy clarifies TTI&rsquo;s legitimate option. It may propose that a boundary crossing transforms an
      observed variable twice back to its starting value while another variable accumulates. A minimal update
      might be \((x,r)\mapsto(Px,r+f[x])\), as the residue ledger states. After two steps, \(x\) can return
      while \(r\) changes by \(f[x]+f[Px]\).</p>
    <p>But the analogy cannot identify \(r\). Human memory works because brains contain changing physical
      records; fatigue has metabolism as a carrier; elapsed time is read by a clock. A cosmic residue likewise
      requires a degree of freedom with units, dynamics and coupling to an instrument. If \(r\) can never
      affect an observation, adding it has no testable consequence. If it does affect observations, the model
      must specify when and by how much.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      (x,r)\xrightarrow{\;P\;}(Px,r+f[x])
      \xrightarrow{\;P\;}(x,r+f[x]+f[Px]).
    \]</p>
    <p>This equation is a template rather than a derivation. It makes the additional assumption explicit and
      offers a clean cancellation case: if \(f[Px]=-f[x]\), no net residue remains after the pair. A theory
      must predict the function rather than choosing it after seeing the result.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">What a decisive comparison would report</p>
    <h2 id="t-measurement-ledger">Measure Return Error, Phase and Environment Separately</h2>
    <p>The final-state error asks whether polarisation failed to return. Interferometric phase asks whether the
      test path differs from a reference. Environmental monitoring asks whether temperature, vibration,
      scattering or uncontrolled modes stored the history. Reporting only output intensity mixes these
      possibilities and cannot establish residue.</p>
    <p>The order of operations should also be reversed. A genuine non-commuting path effect can depend on
      whether \(A\) precedes \(B\), whereas an exact repeated involution has a fixed prediction. Varying
      wavelength, power and temperature can reveal ordinary dispersion or hysteresis. Blocking the reference
      arm tests whether the claimed signal actually required interference.</p>
    <p>A null result is informative. If calibrated optics, measured phase and environmental coupling account
      for all observations, the special TTI residue model has no remaining work to do in that experiment. A
      non-zero unexplained phase would justify further optical investigation, but the leap to cosmic memory
      would still require the same law to connect laboratory and cosmological scales.</p>
    <p>One further subtlety is that states can be equivalent for one measurement while differing for another.
      Multiplying a lone quantum state by a global phase does not change its direct measurement probabilities,
      but a relative phase between two coherent paths can change interference. The reference arm is therefore
      part of the experiment that makes phase observable. Saying “the state returned” must specify which
      equivalence relation and which measurements are intended.</p>
    <p>The same discipline applies to signs. An arrow reversed twice returns visibly. A spinor can require a
      \(4\pi\) rotation for its state vector to return, while observable rays treat a global sign as
      equivalent unless interference exposes it. These are well-defined transformation laws, not evidence that
      all double turns store a universal remainder. The chosen representation determines what counts as return.</p>
    <p>Accordingly, the experiment should publish its full modelled state space before data collection. Adding
      an unmeasured coordinate only after a null result would immunise the hypothesis. Adding a coordinate in
      advance, together with the instrument that reads it and the predicted scale, creates a claim that can
      succeed or fail.</p>
    <p class="muted">The disciplined answer to the title question is therefore: two exact inversions do not
      force a surviving polarity. Path memory can survive in an enlarged, measurable state. The difference
      between those sentences is the difference between algebra and a research programme.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">An experiment that can say no</p>
    <h2 id="t-experiment">Separate Return Error from Path Memory</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Prepare a known polarization state and retain a coherent reference arm.</li>
      <li>Apply a calibrated order-two polarization transformation twice in the test arm.</li>
      <li>Use full polarimetry to measure final-state error, not intensity alone.</li>
      <li>Interfere test and reference arms to measure any relative geometric or dynamic phase.</li>
      <li>Repeat in reversed order and over wavelength, temperature and power to expose non-ideal memory.</li>
      <li>Declare the TTI residue model unsuccessful if every result is accounted for by calibrated optics, noise and environment.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A positive phase measurement would establish an optical path effect. It would not by itself establish
      cosmic inheritance, consciousness, matter&ndash;antimatter conversion or a Möbius universe.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <div class="page-head tight"><p class="kicker">Categories</p><h2 id="t-categories">What Kind of Claim Is Being Made?</h2></div>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Topological</h3><p>Non-orientability and frame return after two circuits are mathematical.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Physical</h3><p>Jones matrices, retardance, polarimetry and geometric phase belong to optics.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Informational</h3><p>A surviving record requires a carrier and distinguishable states.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Philosophical</h3><p>Return-with-memory is a model of identity through change.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Spiritual</h3><p>Initiation and return may be meaningful analogies, not laboratory conclusions.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Cultural</h3><p>Möbius imagery has an artistic history separate from its mathematical definition.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Earned, chosen, refused</p>
    <h2 id="t-boundary">The Question Survives; the Shortcut Does Not</h2>
    <p><strong>Earned:</strong> exact order-two maps restore the modelled state, and closed optical paths can
      carry measurable relative phase. <strong>Chosen:</strong> asking whether a separately defined residue
      variable can connect those structures to TTI. <strong>Refused:</strong> treating any double inversion,
      any polarization change or any Möbius image as evidence for inherited physical memory.</p>
